00:03:53.679 --> 00:03:55.679
What is ggSQL?

00:03:55.679 --> 00:04:04.679
Well, it's a weird thing that we have built over the course of the last half year or so.

00:04:04.679 --> 00:04:08.679
GG gives it away. It's the grammar of graphics.

00:04:08.679 --> 00:04:12.679
If you're not familiar with that concept, it's what underpins ggplot2.

00:04:12.679 --> 00:04:14.679
There's the GG again.

00:04:14.679 --> 00:04:19.679
And it's a theoretical framework for describing graphics,

00:04:19.679 --> 00:04:22.679
which means that from a programming point of view,

00:04:22.679 --> 00:04:29.679
it's a way to be very structured about how you describe your graphics

00:04:29.679 --> 00:04:32.679
and do it in a composable way

00:04:32.679 --> 00:04:36.679
because it kind of breaks down the concepts of data visualization

00:04:36.679 --> 00:04:39.679
into smaller bits that can be composed.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.679
So there's not something like a scatterplot, a boxplot,

00:04:42.679 --> 00:04:46.679
and these things cannot be like mingled together.

00:04:46.679 --> 00:04:50.679
Everything is just parts, Lego bricks that you can put together.

00:04:50.679 --> 00:04:55.679
And if you're coming from the R world, you know ggplot2.

00:04:55.679 --> 00:04:57.679
It has been widely successful.

00:04:57.679 --> 00:05:01.679
I think it's partly because of this whole idea.

00:05:01.679 --> 00:05:05.679
Once you've rocked the idea of ggplot2, you can build more or less anything.

00:05:05.679 --> 00:05:09.679
You don't have to learn new concepts, and that makes it very powerful.

00:05:09.679 --> 00:05:12.679
The learning curve is a bit steep compared to something like a function

00:05:12.679 --> 00:05:17.679
that's called scatterplot, and you know how to make a scatterplot.

00:05:17.679 --> 00:05:20.679
But it pays dividends, and people really enjoy it.

00:05:20.679 --> 00:05:24.679
So ggplot2 is pretty old.

00:05:24.679 --> 00:05:26.679
We wanted to build something new.

00:05:26.679 --> 00:05:28.679
We also wanted to explore something completely different.

00:05:28.679 --> 00:05:33.679
So that's the last three letters of ggsql.

00:05:33.679 --> 00:05:36.679
That's the SQL part.

00:05:36.679 --> 00:05:41.679
So ggsql is a grammar of graphics for a SQL.

00:05:41.679 --> 00:05:46.679
And it's probably just easier to show it.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:51.679
So this is the front page, and it actually starts with an editable.

00:05:51.679 --> 00:05:56.679
It can run in the browser.

00:05:56.679 --> 00:06:01.679
If you're familiar with SQL, hopefully this looks not too unfamiliar,

00:06:01.679 --> 00:06:03.679
at least the first part up here.

00:06:03.679 --> 00:06:05.679
That's pure SQL.

00:06:05.679 --> 00:06:09.679
Everything below that is ggsql.

00:06:09.679 --> 00:06:13.679
And what we have tried to do is to express the ideas

00:06:13.679 --> 00:06:19.679
around the grammar of graphics in a way that feels very native to SQL.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:25.679
So even though we have come up with new concepts and new clauses and so on,

00:06:25.679 --> 00:06:28.679
the hope is that if you're used to SQL,

00:06:28.679 --> 00:06:32.679
then these things and the way they work together would feel familiar

00:06:32.679 --> 00:06:37.679
and at least feel ergonomic to the language.

00:06:37.679 --> 00:06:41.679
So here we start by creating some data.

00:06:41.679 --> 00:06:43.679
We have some built-in data sets.

00:06:43.679 --> 00:06:47.679
Our lovely penguins data set is there, so we can toy with that.

00:06:47.679 --> 00:06:50.679
So we just select everything from penguins,

00:06:50.679 --> 00:06:54.679
where island is this Abisko island.

00:06:54.679 --> 00:06:57.679
And then usually you would get a table back from this,

00:06:57.679 --> 00:07:00.679
but here we just funnel it into the next clause,

00:07:00.679 --> 00:07:03.679
which is this visualize clause.

00:07:03.679 --> 00:07:07.679
It looks kind of like a select clause if you're familiar with SQL.

00:07:07.679 --> 00:07:12.679
We visualize build length as X and build depth as Y.

00:07:12.679 --> 00:07:16.679
This means that we are mapping these columns in the table

00:07:16.679 --> 00:07:20.679
into these abstract ideas of what exists in the plot.

00:07:20.679 --> 00:07:22.679
So X and Y are things in the plot, right?

00:07:22.679 --> 00:07:25.679
And we say, well, build length is going to be X

00:07:25.679 --> 00:07:27.679
and build depth is going to be Y,

00:07:27.679 --> 00:07:31.679
and body mass is going to be the fill color, which is just called fill.

00:07:31.679 --> 00:07:33.679
And then we begin to compose it.

00:07:33.679 --> 00:07:36.679
So now we have how the data relates to everything,

00:07:36.679 --> 00:07:38.679
and now we can begin to compose it.

00:07:38.679 --> 00:07:40.679
We can say we'll draw points,

00:07:40.679 --> 00:07:43.679
and we'll just inherit all of these things we have up here.

00:07:43.679 --> 00:07:49.679
So if we just, like, let's just delete all of that.

00:07:49.679 --> 00:07:52.679
So if we just draw points, we have the points, we have the fill.

00:07:52.679 --> 00:07:54.679
It already gives us a lot for free.

00:07:54.679 --> 00:07:58.679
It gives us this legend and so on and so forth.

00:07:59.679 --> 00:08:06.679
We could do things like draw smooth,

00:08:06.679 --> 00:08:10.679
and it will layer on top of that an additional layer,

00:08:10.679 --> 00:08:15.679
which is a smoothing curve through all of these points.

00:08:15.679 --> 00:08:20.679
We can see that there might be some different structures in there.

00:08:20.679 --> 00:08:32.679
So we could say draw smooth partition by species probably, right?

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:36.679
And we'll get to, so we group the data into two things,

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:39.679
and we get a line from each of them.

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:45.679
So this concept, like, it's a lot of code

00:08:45.679 --> 00:08:47.679
if you just want to have a scatterplot, right?

00:08:47.679 --> 00:08:51.679
But you can see that this concept naturally extends to

00:08:51.679 --> 00:08:54.679
basically whatever you want to plot.

00:08:54.679 --> 00:08:57.679
If we're not interested in points at all,

00:08:57.679 --> 00:09:01.679
we can say let's draw something else.

00:09:01.679 --> 00:09:08.679
We'll say species as X.

00:09:13.679 --> 00:09:16.679
This is unrehearsed, true to the style.

00:09:16.679 --> 00:09:22.679
And we'll draw a bar, and we'll have a bar plot with automatic count.

00:09:24.679 --> 00:09:27.679
So you can see that there is a lot of flexibility

00:09:27.679 --> 00:09:31.679
in some ideas that are universal to plots, right?

00:09:31.679 --> 00:09:35.679
And this is the thing that underpins ggplot2,

00:09:35.679 --> 00:09:37.679
and now we're doing it for SQL.

00:09:39.679 --> 00:09:41.679
Why do we do that?

00:09:41.679 --> 00:09:44.679
Well, we think we don't want to keep ggplot2

00:09:44.679 --> 00:09:49.679
and the fantastic ideas behind that to only our users.

00:09:49.679 --> 00:09:53.679
So that's one of the things we want to engage with a new audience.

00:09:53.679 --> 00:09:59.679
In Puzzit, we have not spent too much time catering to SQL users,

00:09:59.679 --> 00:10:03.679
but SQL is such an integral part of data science

00:10:03.679 --> 00:10:05.679
that it feels wrong not to.

00:10:05.679 --> 00:10:08.679
And it's also like we feel like there's a gap there

00:10:08.679 --> 00:10:13.679
in their workflows that when you're a heavy SQL users,

00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:16.679
if you want to look at the data, usually you would have to export it,

00:10:16.679 --> 00:10:19.679
put it into Excel, put it into Python, put it into R,

00:10:19.679 --> 00:10:22.679
do all of that context switching just for the sake

00:10:22.679 --> 00:10:26.679
of looking at some interesting part of your data.

00:10:26.679 --> 00:10:30.679
So why not engage with them in a more direct manner?

