I'm not here to like kind of convince you that you should change your ways.

00:16:20.679 --> 00:16:25.679
But there is like a huge amount of people that are, I would say,

00:16:25.679 --> 00:16:30.679
starved for good visualization options because they do not work in R or Python.

00:16:30.679 --> 00:16:37.679
They work in SQL and they will then have to put it into a GUI or some sort of thing.

00:16:37.679 --> 00:16:40.679
And in order to see their data.

00:16:40.679 --> 00:16:43.679
And I think we can do better for them.

00:16:43.679 --> 00:16:49.679
I also think that there are like, there are technical good reasons to reach for GSQL,

00:16:49.679 --> 00:16:53.679
even if you're working in R or Python.

00:16:53.679 --> 00:17:00.679
One of the things is that GSQL is structured in a way that means that like every plot is,

00:17:00.679 --> 00:17:08.680
like if you have a billion data points and want to create a, let's say a histogram of that,

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:16.680
you would have to like take all of that into R and do segmentation and so on to do it in ggplot2.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:24.680
With GGSQL, what it does is actually like it constructs a SQL query to only get back the data required to plot it.

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:31.680
So all of the statistical transformation, even for something like density plot or box plots and all of these things,

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:35.680
they all happen in your high powered SQL database.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:37.680
And it's very, very fast.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:41.680
So for some situations, especially with large data warehouses,

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:47.680
like I think it's a much more economic way to do data exploration.

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:54.680
I like the idea of keeping your EDA inside of SQL and adding visualizations to it.

