Or can they be intermingled somehow as far as like having SQL code interspersed with GGSQL code?

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:29.680
Yeah, so if you have a GGSQL file, then you can like have delimiters that like break them up in chunks.

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:34.680
We're still like working on how the best way to do that is.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:39.680
So that's a kind of a weak answer.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:43.680
But yes, you can. I don't think it's perfect right now.

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:52.680
We are missing some infrastructure to make the feeling in Positron even better.

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:58.680
We don't have a pure language server that understands GGSQL and can say statement ends here.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:03.680
So when you say command enter, it executes down to that line and so on.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:11.680
So some of the affordances that we're used to with Python and R is still like on the building block in the pipeline.

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:17.680
All right. Well, I have so many more questions.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:23.680
I would love us to keep going, but I will ask one more, which I think could be pretty quick.

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:29.680
It's from Nessa, and it says, can I use something like this with a really big data platform like Snowflake?

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:31.680
Is that a main use case?

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:35.680
Yes, it's a very main use case.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:47.680
Basically, what we have now is we have a modular setup for GGSQL, which consists of a reader module, a plot module, which is kind of the central thing, and a writer module.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:50.680
The reader module is the one that interacts with your database.

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:56.680
And it's modular. It has DuckDB bindings. It has ODBC bindings.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:01.680
Through ODBC, it has a dedicated Snowflake binding as well.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:07.680
And we're going to add so many interesting backends to it.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:12.680
Kind of what we said, we want to meet people where their data are.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.680
And that's where we're going with it.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:30.680
On the other side, there's this writer module, which is the one that takes all of these queries that end up in the data that should be plotted and converts it to something that is actually a plot.

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:33.680
And currently, we only have one writer, which is a Megalite writer.

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:42.680
So it takes all of these things and writes it into a Megalite spec, which is like a JSON spec, and then renders it.

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:47.680
But this is not the end of the line for writers.

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:50.680
We're working on something much more exciting.

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:57.680
But it allowed us to move very fast because we didn't have to think about the actual rendering.

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:03.680
Yeah. Well, let's go ahead and keep going with a little bit more demonstration.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:05.680
And I'm going to hold on to some of these questions.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:07.680
Thank you, everybody, for asking them.

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:14.680
They have been amazing questions that are getting at things that I would love to know, too.

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:16.680
Let's see.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:18.680
Let me look at my notes here.

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:32.680
One of the questions that I had, you touched on it just a little bit with the visualization part, is like, if I wanted to use this, do I need to be in a platform that has a visualization tool like a viewer?

00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:38.680
Because a lot of people might be like, oh, can I just pop into SQLite 3 and use this?

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:41.680
Is it going to open up like a graphics viewer of some kind?

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:43.680
Yes.

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:47.680
So we have a DuckDB extension.

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:56.680
So if you're working in DuckDB, you can say install ggSQL and you can immediately use ggSQL from within DuckDB.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:22:01.680
I think it's always interesting to figure out how to integrate.

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:10.680
I think what we want to do with Positron is to create the premier SQL platform, this premier SQL IDE.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:21.680
So it's going to be the obvious way to use this, but we don't want to make it monolithic in the sense that you have to use Positron to get a nice experience with this.

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:26.680
So one of this is like a DuckDB extension for it.

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:29.680
It obviously doesn't have a viewer pane.

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:36.680
So what it does is it's popping up a browser window and displays it there.

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:39.680
And that's kind of the thing.

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:52.680
You can be a bit constrained when you're dealing with extensions for specific SQL engines, but hopefully we can meet people in a lot of places.

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:58.680
Marimo is another interesting place to see this.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:03.680
We don't have anything for it right now, but I think that's an obvious thing to explore.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:10.680
And then there's Quarto documents with the ggSQL kernel.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:13.680
I don't even think of Marimo plus SQL.

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:16.680
I just think of it as Python.

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:21.680
And I know that it can play well with Quarto.

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:26.680
We had a couple more questions that if you have time to answer them would be amazing.

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:37.680
So Nathan had asked, I can see a future thing like dbplyr where ggplot2 just has a SQL backend and runs ggSQL.

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:39.680
If you give it a database.

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:42.680
So is that a direction y'all are thinking of going?

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:47.680
Not exactly.

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:48.680
I don't think so.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:24:04.680
I think for all of their similarities, there are also strong dissimilarities between the way ggplot2 works and how it expresses itself and how ggSQL works.

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:13.680
Part of it is some of these chains of old depth that we want to shed, which means there's not a direct translation.

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:26.680
So I think it's more natural to think that we would in some way provide a good high level API for this in R.

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:35.680
That's not ggplot2, but just like kind of what dbplyr does constructs a query that is then executed.

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:38.680
I think that's a better approach.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:42.680
There's already like a ggSQL R package with bindings for ggSQL.

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:53.680
So it doesn't have a high level interface like you just give it a SQL string, but you can use that to actually create your own readers.

00:24:53.680 --> 00:25:03.680
So if you have some data source in R that you cannot interact with with ggSQL, then you can use R, the R package as a binding between that and quickly get going.

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:05.680
We have the same for Python as well.

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:07.680
We have a ggSQL Python package.

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:16.680
This also kind of shows that we're trying to put this in as many places as we possibly can.

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:21.680
One interesting thing about the R package is that it's also a knitter engine.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:32.680
So you can use ggSQL in knitter and then use that to mix R, Python, and ggSQL code in a single quarter document.

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:43.680
And ggSQL automatically gets access to the datasets that you have in both your R and Python chunks in front of it.

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:57.680
So that's a very smooth way of saying, I'm using Python or R to do my data wrangling, and then I'm going to use ggSQL to describe my plots and just automatically get access to whatever data you create.

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:02.680
That is so super cool.

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:08.680
I think that Isabella has a question for you.

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:21.680
I'm just curious, would you mind showing us, say, we have a lot of folks who are new to the SQL world and particularly coming from the R, Python world.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:25.680
What would it look like to, you mentioned the Positron extension.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:31.680
After you install the extension, what do you do to use ggSQL within Positron?

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:37.680
Sure, let's get back to this view.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:42.680
So let's assume that I'm here, I am an R developer.

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:48.680
I was also curious, what is the .rs file? I've never seen that before.

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:52.680
Oh, this is a Rust file.

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.680
So ggSQL is written in Rust.

00:26:54.680 --> 00:27:05.680
It's pure Rust, which means that it compiles to WebAssembly, which is also why we can have it in our website and executing things.

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:10.680
It's not talking to a server, it's just running in your browser, doing interesting stuff.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:13.680
Anyway, you're here.

00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:21.680
You have installed the ggSQL extension.

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:25.680
Can everybody see Thomas' size? Does it need to be a little bit bigger?

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:29.680
We might do one zoom in, one command plus sign.

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:31.680
There we go.

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:34.680
For the YouTube crowd, yes, thank you.

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:39.680
So you have installed this, you have installed ggSQL as well.

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:47.680
It's kind of like with Quoto, you can install the extension, but you also need to install Quoto so that the executable exists in your system.

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:53.680
So if you go to the website and go to get started, there's install instructions there.

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:55.680
It's just a regular installer.

00:27:55.680 --> 00:28:00.680
Anyway, so you have all this set up, and now you want to do something.

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:11.680
What you can do is, if you want to get back to this console that I had before, we can do this by saying start another.

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:22.680
And then you can see that it exists there as a console engine, and we're up and running.

00:28:22.680 --> 00:28:27.680
And then you can just do whatever, as I did before.

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:36.680
If you have data sources in your connection pane, you can have them set up and connect to them again.

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:40.680
Resume connection, and you can begin to explore.

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:52.680
So this console, while it's a ggSQL console, can also execute regular SQL.

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:57.680
So you can do both, one and the other.

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:00.680
We don't have tab completion yet.

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:03.680
This is one of the things, one of the four instances that we're looking into.

