I will just...

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:08.680
Lego cars.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:10.680
There you go.

00:29:12.828 --> 00:29:21.948
So you can do that, and then you can, let's say, what's a meaningful

00:29:23.788 --> 00:29:25.388
way to describe this?

00:29:25.388 --> 00:29:33.307
Which visualize is trans.

00:29:42.748 --> 00:29:46.988
Super interested in where this dataset lives as well.

00:29:46.988 --> 00:30:01.148
This is my PostgreSQL dataset of Lego colors.

00:30:01.148 --> 00:30:11.707
So this is me using ggsql to run a query on an ODBC-backed PostgreSQL database, and

00:30:11.948 --> 00:30:19.148
I'm getting a horrible plot out of it, but anyway, the core idea is there.

00:30:21.307 --> 00:30:27.707
And you can see that, for instance, Lego sets, that's a larger thing.

00:30:28.748 --> 00:30:36.267
We also have, so this connection pane is driven by the ggsql extension, but we also integrate

00:30:36.267 --> 00:30:38.188
into the data viewer as well.

00:30:38.188 --> 00:30:45.067
So you have all of these things that you're used to looking at when you're in ggsql world

00:30:45.067 --> 00:30:46.108
and positron as well.

00:30:48.508 --> 00:30:52.508
Yeah, so I think that's how you'd get started, I think.

00:30:52.508 --> 00:31:00.347
If you want to get started just toying around and seeing if this really is something for

00:31:00.428 --> 00:31:12.027
you, then we also have a no setup solution on the website, which is, let's see, the playground,

00:31:12.908 --> 00:31:17.707
which is somewhat of an IDE that exists in your browser.

00:31:18.748 --> 00:31:23.627
You can upload your own data to play with it, but you can also use some of the built-in

00:31:23.627 --> 00:31:30.428
datasets, and just like there are examples, example plots that you can start off and then

00:31:30.428 --> 00:31:33.707
just begin to do fun stuff with it.

00:31:34.668 --> 00:31:39.388
This is so fun, because you could still just use basic SQL in here, right?

00:31:39.388 --> 00:31:43.228
You could just play around with SQL.

00:31:43.228 --> 00:31:47.627
This is a good little chance to learn if anybody is curious.

00:31:48.267 --> 00:31:48.767
Yeah.

00:31:49.567 --> 00:31:54.448
In terms of the SQL that you wrote in positron, can it do all the SQL?

00:31:56.207 --> 00:31:56.707
Yes.

00:31:56.928 --> 00:31:57.428
Wow.

00:32:00.528 --> 00:32:05.567
So the way it works is that it's basically just taking your SQL statement and sending

00:32:05.567 --> 00:32:06.368
it to your backend.

00:32:06.368 --> 00:32:08.127
So you're constrained by your backend.

00:32:08.127 --> 00:32:10.368
You cannot use DuckDB functions.

00:32:10.368 --> 00:32:17.728
If you have a PostgreSQL database, then you're limited to that and vice versa.

00:32:19.088 --> 00:32:25.567
So whatever SQL your data, whatever dialect your data lives in, you can use that.

00:32:27.808 --> 00:32:34.127
We had a question from Notobeko that said, have you discovered anything that this fusion

00:32:34.127 --> 00:32:41.168
of SQL and ggplot can do that is unique to it past what the individual parts alongside

00:32:41.168 --> 00:32:42.528
one another can do?

00:32:43.487 --> 00:32:51.008
And I would further this by saying, have you hit the limits of it where you're like, it's

00:32:51.008 --> 00:32:52.607
good for this, but it's not good for this?

00:32:53.567 --> 00:32:54.067
Yeah.

00:32:56.767 --> 00:32:57.808
That's a good question.

00:32:58.528 --> 00:33:14.047
I think this, where SQL and gg shines together is more on like a theoretical, like foundational

00:33:14.047 --> 00:33:14.547
level.

00:33:16.767 --> 00:33:18.607
It feels very right.

00:33:18.607 --> 00:33:23.567
Like the first time someone told me about this was like, I didn't come up with this

00:33:23.567 --> 00:33:24.607
idea, but I took it on.

00:33:25.168 --> 00:33:28.767
The first time it was presented to me, it was like, that sounds weird.

00:33:29.568 --> 00:33:34.928
But SQL is declarative data manipulation.

00:33:35.568 --> 00:33:39.487
gg, grammar of graphics, is declarative data visualization.

00:33:39.487 --> 00:33:46.528
And like these two things just feel so right together in the same sense that dplyr and

00:33:46.528 --> 00:33:49.648
ggplot2 also feels kind of right together.

00:33:51.887 --> 00:33:54.287
But this is like, this is on a different level.

00:33:54.528 --> 00:33:58.688
It's like purely the syntax of SQL.

00:33:58.688 --> 00:34:02.928
It's very verbose, but it also reads like super nice.

00:34:04.688 --> 00:34:11.248
And what we have found also, if you're using like LLMs to generate your code, like it picks

00:34:11.248 --> 00:34:14.287
up gg SQL syntax, like very easy.

00:34:14.287 --> 00:34:21.007
And it's very good at expressing itself with it because it reads like text.

00:34:21.808 --> 00:34:28.047
And it has like, it tries to stay as true to SQL, which it has a lot of, like there's

00:34:28.047 --> 00:34:31.248
a lot of SQL in LLMs training data.

00:34:32.047 --> 00:34:34.767
And it tries to stay as true to that, like ether.

00:34:34.767 --> 00:34:37.408
So it like picks it up really easy.

00:34:39.887 --> 00:34:42.528
Yeah, so, sorry, go ahead.

00:34:42.528 --> 00:34:46.928
No, no, I was going to say, I think that that covers the first part.

00:34:46.928 --> 00:34:51.727
And I was going to ask, I was going to say, can I like rephrase my question?

00:34:51.727 --> 00:34:53.887
Because I said, have you reached the limits of it?

00:34:54.607 --> 00:34:59.968
I guess what I'm really saying is like, could you talk a little bit about what alpha means?

00:34:59.968 --> 00:35:01.167
What being an alpha means?

00:35:02.207 --> 00:35:06.128
Because have you reached the limits is definitely not a challenge.

00:35:06.128 --> 00:35:10.607
It's more like, hey, let's talk about where the state of things right now, where we hope

00:35:10.607 --> 00:35:13.568
they will go and how people can help give feedback too.

00:35:14.047 --> 00:35:19.088
Yeah, I can start by saying we haven't reached the limit, but we have, I will entertain that

00:35:19.088 --> 00:35:27.007
idea a bit first, because it does like trying to be true to the SQL syntax and the SQL field

00:35:27.727 --> 00:35:32.767
also provides us with some limits in what we can do to express ourselves.

00:35:33.568 --> 00:35:39.167
And one of them is like, in R and Python, you can just like make up functions and pass

00:35:39.167 --> 00:35:42.207
them into other functions.

00:35:42.207 --> 00:35:51.088
So like if you wanted to quickly make a summary function, you'll pass it into the stat summary

00:35:53.327 --> 00:35:55.808
layer and you can do all sorts of things.

00:35:55.808 --> 00:35:56.607
We're limited here.

00:35:56.607 --> 00:36:03.968
We don't want people like, it's not even possible in a sensible way to create functions on the

00:36:03.968 --> 00:36:04.848
fly.

00:36:04.848 --> 00:36:10.207
So we have to create a syntax that is maximally expressive without resorting to these cheap

00:36:10.207 --> 00:36:10.707
tricks.

00:36:11.648 --> 00:36:20.607
And that is limiting, but it also forces us to think like very clearly around the syntax.

00:36:20.607 --> 00:36:25.327
There is no escape hatch of just passing the function and like you deal with it.

00:36:25.327 --> 00:36:26.287
Like we can't do that.

00:36:26.287 --> 00:36:28.128
We have to think all these things through.

00:36:28.128 --> 00:36:30.287
So that's also been very interesting.

00:36:32.447 --> 00:36:33.727
What does alpha mean?

00:36:33.727 --> 00:36:38.287
It means that you should probably not build a company around it yet.

00:36:38.767 --> 00:36:45.568
I think I am like, you could call it better or alpha.

00:36:45.568 --> 00:36:47.808
I don't put too much into this.

00:36:47.808 --> 00:36:53.088
It's to me, it just means that it's unfinished and it's subject to change if we find out

00:36:53.088 --> 00:36:55.568
that something doesn't work as we want it to.

00:36:57.167 --> 00:37:05.648
In terms of polish, I think we are way beyond the alpha mark there in terms of what it can

00:37:05.648 --> 00:37:07.007
do and so on.

00:37:07.327 --> 00:37:12.287
I think we have superseded what we set out to do by many, many leaks.

00:37:13.727 --> 00:37:20.848
So alpha is just us being honest that it's still a very, very active development and

00:37:20.848 --> 00:37:23.088
we're still feeling our way around it.

00:37:25.167 --> 00:37:29.808
But as you can see, I think from the website as well, I don't want to rewrite all of this

00:37:32.207 --> 00:37:33.007
documentation.

00:37:33.088 --> 00:37:39.167
So we're staying pretty, it's fairly solid as it is right now.

00:37:39.968 --> 00:37:45.248
And I think if you learn it now, you will not wake up in six months and see it being

00:37:45.248 --> 00:37:49.167
changed drastically and you have to relearn it in any way.

00:37:51.167 --> 00:37:53.568
There are things that we really want to get in.

00:37:53.568 --> 00:37:55.568
So this is spatial stuff.

00:37:56.128 --> 00:38:00.047
It's one of these things that will be part of the next release.

00:38:00.768 --> 00:38:01.268
Oh, yeah.

00:38:03.487 --> 00:38:12.368
So Jian, my co-developer, has been working on this for a month or so with very rich spatial

00:38:12.368 --> 00:38:20.368
support and support for all of these crazy projections and sort of things.

00:38:20.607 --> 00:38:27.727
That also presents us with some interesting, let's say, challenges, because usually when

00:38:27.727 --> 00:38:33.407
you have SQL data, you don't have borders of countries, right?

00:38:33.407 --> 00:38:38.448
You have a country and you have some value for it, and you want to make a chloropleth

00:38:38.448 --> 00:38:39.487
map or whatever.

00:38:41.248 --> 00:38:49.248
So how in R, it's more normal to like, well, you just like, you know, you have a lot of

00:38:49.248 --> 00:38:54.047
data, well, you just like download some data from natural earth, and then you do left join

00:38:54.047 --> 00:38:56.128
and you'll have your like all of these kind of things.

00:38:56.928 --> 00:39:05.808
And working with spatial data is, I think, even more foreign for your regular pure SQL

00:39:05.808 --> 00:39:10.688
user, because spatial data is usually not part of a database, right?

00:39:11.167 --> 00:39:20.287
So how do we, without making cheap excuses and polluting the syntax with

00:39:23.647 --> 00:39:31.328
unpure escape hatches, how do we provide a nice experience for people that are not super

00:39:31.328 --> 00:39:37.568
into spatial data, but want to make a chloropleth map, for instance, or want to plot location

00:39:37.568 --> 00:39:43.328
of instances on the globe, like earthquake centers or whatever, like these kind of things.

00:39:44.527 --> 00:39:45.888
So that's pretty interesting.

00:39:45.888 --> 00:39:47.888
We're still feeling a way around that.

00:39:48.688 --> 00:39:49.087
All right.

00:39:49.087 --> 00:39:53.808
Well, I think that the questions that we have rolling in can lead us in a cool direction.

00:39:53.808 --> 00:39:55.808
So let me do a couple of things.

